":["Daedalic Entertainment"],"released":"May 12, 2014","meta":72,"langs":["English","French","Italian","German","Spanish - Spain"],"min":[["OS","Windows Vista / 7"],["Processor","Intel Dual-Core 2.6 GHz / AMD Dual-Core Athlon 3.0 GHz"],["Memory","2 GB RAM"],["Graphics","SM 3.0 with 512MB VRAM; NVIDIA GeForce 8500 GT / AMD Radeon HD 4650 or greater"],["DirectX","Version 9.0c"],["Storage","2 GB available space"],["Sound Card","DirectX Compatible"]],"rec":[["OS","Windows 7 or higher"],["Processor","Quad-Core Processor"],["Memory","4 GB RAM"],["Graphics","SM 3.0 with 1GB VRAM; NVIDIA GeForce GTX 280 / AMD Radeon HD 4830 or greater"],["DirectX","Version 9.0c"],["Storage","2 GB available space"],["Sound Card","DirectX Compatible"]],"deck":1,"blurb":"Koru is a young boy in Colortown, a city built on creativity and emotion, where a dark force called the Bleakness is draining color and joy from the world. You guide him through a 3D action adventure, using color and emotion to bring the city back to life. Despite a style that recalls 1990s mascot platformers, the game has no jump button at all.\n\nDeveloper Mimimi Games cites Jak and Daxter and Banjo-Kazooie as touchstones, and the tone is bright, colorful and family-friendly. It is a single-player game with full controller support that works for playing alongside children or for adults who want a lighthearted throwback."}}